---
/**
 * The header lockup, with our own markup.
 *
 * Starlight's version does the same job — a light/dark `<img>` pair plus a visually-hidden title — and its 404
 * bytes of scoped CSS were being fought rather than used: `native.css` gives every `img` a `height: auto` in the
 * `cs-native` layer, which is ordered after `starlight.core`, so Starlight's logo sizing lost and `docs.css` had
 * to size the lockup itself. Owning the markup means that rule is this site's own decision rather than a
 * counterweight.
 *
 * The scheme swap uses `cs-light`/`cs-dark` — the classes the library's own themes key off, which `theme-sync.js`
 * keeps in step with Starlight's `data-theme` — rather than Starlight's `light:sl-hidden`/`dark:sl-hidden`
 * utilities. Those live in `util.css`, which `Page.astro` imports and this site cannot remove, so using them
 * would have kept a dependency for no gain. There is no `cs-*` utility for "show only in one colour scheme",
 * which is a gap worth noting: the swap takes two rules in `docs.css`.
 *
 * `print:hidden` on the dark copy is kept for the reason `Pagination` keeps it — `print.css` is what makes the
 * printed page use the light lockup, and that stylesheet is not removable.
 *
 * The intrinsic `width`/`height` stay on both images so the header reserves its space before they load. The
 * `<span>` keeps the site title for assistive technology, since the lockup carries the name as artwork.
 */
import light from '../assets/cds-mark-light.png';
import dark from '../assets/cds-mark-dark.png';

const { siteTitle, siteTitleHref } = Astro.locals.starlightRoute;
---

<a href={siteTitleHref} class="site-title cs-cluster cs-gap-s">
  <img class="site-logo light-only" alt="" src={light.src} width={light.width} height={light.height} />
  <img
    class="site-logo dark-only print:hidden"
    alt=""
    src={dark.src}
    width={dark.width}
    height={dark.height}
  />
  <span class="cs-visually-hidden" translate="no">{siteTitle}</span>
</a>
